> The immediate concern I have with C++ is that a modded source would need
> to go throw an inconvenient, platform dependant recompile. What I want
> to achieve is the ability to download a platform independant file e.g.
> for a new artifact, put it in a directory, and run NH. An interpreted or
> bytecode interpreted language would be needed for this.

Ok, platform independence is a good goal. I can start studying Python again.
As long as it has facilities sufficient to implement the various design
patterns, it should be fine.

Though the idea of being able to just drop a file in a directory, and get a
new object in the game raises interesting dilemmas:

What do you do if you load, say, Trollsbane into a game with no trolls? Let
the item be useless?
Throw an exception? Let the author decide?

How does the new item participate in the random generation of items?

> Java has a performance penalty for calling native code, and the Swing
> GUI library is implemented in Java, thus slower than Python's bindings.

Just out of curiosity, were you thinking of abandoning the text interface?

>> I'd probably need to
>> study the way the existing code works before seriously starting a new
>> design, but perhaps not.
> 
> IMO the many detailed spoilers we have could function as a good design
> doc.

A requirements doc, yes, but not a design doc. A requirements doc says WHAT
the program should do, a design doc says HOW the program does it. "The
level creator uses a factory to obtain objects to place on the levels it
creates.", later "The level creator calls ItemFactory.get(ItemClass) to get
an item of the given ItemClass" ,etc. (probably not worded like that, but
you get the idea).
 
> I've seen enough of the NH code to think "eww, icky!" and want to ignore
> it completely.

Fair enough, I remember having that feeling often when I did look through
the source code a while ago.

>> Anyone else out there interested in the exercise?
> 
> I suspect someone will have to take the lead, and produce a working
> demo. Once that is done, people may start volunteering.

I think we'd be better off designing the thing first, save the demo for
later. It's not as cool, but time spent on design up front can save endless
refactorings later.



